1
00:00:05,336 --> 00:00:06,774
- Good afternoon. (audience clapping)

2
00:00:06,774 --> 00:00:09,053
How's everyone feeling after another round

3
00:00:09,053 --> 00:00:10,151
of breakout sessions?

4
00:00:10,151 --> 00:00:13,011
(audience cheering) (audience clapping)

5
00:00:13,011 --> 00:00:16,620
Thank you all so much for participating today and yesterday,

6
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:19,200
and all of the dialogue and conversations.

7
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,973
It's been an incredible lineup of ideas so far,

8
00:00:21,973 --> 00:00:23,670
and we have more to share

9
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:26,722
as we close out our Futures Summit 2025.

10
00:00:26,722 --> 00:00:30,513
This afternoon, we'll focus on the theme of creativity.

11
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,130
We'll focus on how AI can spark new ways of thinking,

12
00:00:35,130 --> 00:00:37,418
learning, and imagining the future.

13
00:00:37,418 --> 00:00:42,086
We're excited to share, first, a student-created short film

14
00:00:42,086 --> 00:00:45,546
that amplifies their emerging voices and perspectives,

15
00:00:45,546 --> 00:00:49,440
reminding us that the story of AI is

16
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:51,960
as much about human creativity

17
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:54,240
as it is about the technology.

18
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:57,960
So let's take a look at, "Just Take AIla,"

19
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:01,623
directed by students, Reyva Dhillon and Koloa Tonga.

20
00:01:03,463 --> 00:01:06,630
(audience applauding)

21
00:01:10,176 --> 00:01:12,926
(birds chirping)

22
00:01:15,072 --> 00:01:16,721
(bouncy music)

23
00:01:16,721 --> 00:01:18,450
- [Narrator] Has this ever happened to you?

24
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:20,700
You no longer have to worry

25
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:23,700
about pesky little mistakes ruining your day.

26
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:24,615
Just take AIla,

27
00:01:24,615 --> 00:01:27,510
the Artificial Intelligence Learning Assistant,

28
00:01:27,510 --> 00:01:30,628
so you never have to worry about human error again.

29
00:01:30,628 --> 00:01:33,554
This innovative technology requires a single oral dose

30
00:01:33,554 --> 00:01:35,730
and through cutting edge neural transmission,

31
00:01:35,730 --> 00:01:38,310
you'll have your very own personal assistant.

32
00:01:38,310 --> 00:01:39,600
Stressed about the big meeting?

33
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,102
Just take AIla.

34
00:01:41,102 --> 00:01:43,560
AIla will learn everything about your schedule

35
00:01:43,560 --> 00:01:44,721
and adapt to your patterns.

36
00:01:44,721 --> 00:01:46,710
Leaving the house and missing something?

37
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:47,972
AIla will take care of that.

38
00:01:47,972 --> 00:01:49,533
Trying to eat healthier?

39
00:01:49,533 --> 00:01:51,480
AIla will make sure you aren't getting tempted

40
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:52,650
by the junk food.

41
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:53,970
Don't decide what to eat, (audience chuckling)

42
00:01:53,970 --> 00:01:55,905
AIla will do that for you.

43
00:01:55,905 --> 00:01:58,362
AIla comes with advanced security protocols

44
00:01:58,362 --> 00:02:00,213
to prioritize user safety.

45
00:02:00,213 --> 00:02:01,479
At any sign of danger,

46
00:02:01,479 --> 00:02:04,892
AIla will transmit an audible warning to her user.

47
00:02:04,892 --> 00:02:07,272
With AIla, you'll never be alone.

48
00:02:07,272 --> 00:02:09,150
(bouncy music)

49
00:02:09,150 --> 00:02:11,190
Why waste time and money on a flawed therapist

50
00:02:11,190 --> 00:02:12,930
when AIla can do it better?

51
00:02:12,930 --> 00:02:14,250
Forget the dating apps,

52
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:17,504
AIla is the perfect best friend, confidant, and partner.

53
00:02:17,504 --> 00:02:21,257
Make every day the easiest day, and just take AIla.

54
00:02:21,257 --> 00:02:23,520
Use of the Artificial Intelligence Learning Assistant,

55
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:25,530
or AIla, may cause cases of cognitive dissonance,

56
00:02:25,530 --> 00:02:27,360
depression, increased paranoia of technology,

57
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:29,340
aversion towards humans beings, social interactions,

58
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:31,650
and the ability to process complex literary texts.

59
00:02:31,650 --> 00:02:32,543
AIla is not intended for those who struggle

60
00:02:32,543 --> 00:02:34,170
with pre-existing mental health conditions,

61
00:02:34,170 --> 00:02:35,003
like schizophrenia.

62
00:02:35,003 --> 00:02:35,836
It's a-

63
00:02:35,836 --> 00:02:36,669
(audience laughing)

64
00:02:36,669 --> 00:02:37,502
- [Participant] What the fuck was that?

65
00:02:38,473 --> 00:02:39,983
(audience laughing)

66
00:02:39,983 --> 00:02:41,970
Can you just change that whole part

67
00:02:41,970 --> 00:02:44,253
and make it into something happier?

68
00:02:45,171 --> 00:02:47,340
And also, the disclaimer part,

69
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:48,923
like, why do we even have that?

70
00:02:48,923 --> 00:02:50,807
I mean, this isn't regulated.

71
00:02:50,807 --> 00:02:51,840
- [Participant] Yeah, I agree.

72
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:53,850
And honestly, try a new version of AIla.

73
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:54,743
- [Participant] All right, yeah,

74
00:02:56,141 --> 00:02:58,069
let's try this again.

75
00:02:58,069 --> 00:03:03,069
(birds chirping) (enchanted music)

76
00:03:05,688 --> 00:03:07,789
- [Narrator] Has this ever happened to you?

77
00:03:07,789 --> 00:03:08,958
You no longer have...

78
00:03:08,958 --> 00:03:11,819
Guys, are you paying attention?

79
00:03:11,819 --> 00:03:13,863
I don't think this is working.

80
00:03:13,863 --> 00:03:16,121
It's really not working.

81
00:03:16,121 --> 00:03:19,500
(enchanted music)

82
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:20,790
Just take AIla,

83
00:03:20,790 --> 00:03:23,310
the Artificial Intelligence Learning Assistant.

84
00:03:23,310 --> 00:03:25,138
So you, hey, are you paying...

85
00:03:25,138 --> 00:03:26,408
Guys?

86
00:03:26,408 --> 00:03:29,223
Can we get some new people to write prompts in here?

87
00:03:29,223 --> 00:03:31,181
Hey, are you paying attention?

88
00:03:31,181 --> 00:03:32,599
I'm not doing this again.

89
00:03:32,599 --> 00:03:35,432
(enchanted music)

90
00:03:42,672 --> 00:03:45,505
(enchanted music)

91
00:03:52,768 --> 00:03:55,601
(enchanted music)

92
00:04:00,687 --> 00:04:03,854
(audience applauding)

93
00:04:06,969 --> 00:04:09,625
- Interesting, right? (all chuckling)

94
00:04:09,625 --> 00:04:12,306
We have to appreciate not just the story,

95
00:04:12,306 --> 00:04:15,236
but the craft and the sentiment behind it.

96
00:04:15,236 --> 00:04:17,040
This film was directed

97
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:18,768
by two of our community college students,

98
00:04:18,768 --> 00:04:21,360
and produced by these amazing students

99
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:23,400
with all the post-production,

100
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:26,279
created using entirely Adobe Creative Tools.

101
00:04:26,279 --> 00:04:30,701
A reminder, again, of how technology can amplify creativity

102
00:04:30,701 --> 00:04:32,934
and bring our ideas to life.

103
00:04:32,934 --> 00:04:35,528
And on that note, it's the perfect time

104
00:04:35,528 --> 00:04:37,680
to welcome our next speaker,

105
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:40,650
someone who knows this intersection of creativity

106
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:42,525
and technology very well.

107
00:04:42,525 --> 00:04:45,087
Our next speaker is Brian Johnsrud.

108
00:04:45,087 --> 00:04:47,909
He's the Director of Educational Thought Leadership

109
00:04:47,909 --> 00:04:48,899
and Student Success at Adobe.

110
00:04:48,899 --> 00:04:50,670
- [Audience Member] Thanks for the job change.

111
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:53,070
- Brian is a former K-12 teacher,

112
00:04:53,070 --> 00:04:56,048
current college lecturer and researcher,

113
00:04:56,048 --> 00:04:59,181
and Thought Leader in educational technology.

114
00:04:59,181 --> 00:05:03,960
He knows and understands our students, and he is passionate

115
00:05:03,960 --> 00:05:07,730
about helping this next generation unlock their creativity

116
00:05:07,730 --> 00:05:11,146
and acquire essential digital literacy skills

117
00:05:11,146 --> 00:05:13,440
and artificial intelligence skills,

118
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:15,000
so that they can be launched

119
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,538
into their future careers and beyond.

120
00:05:17,538 --> 00:05:20,886
Please join me in welcoming Brian Johnsrud

121
00:05:20,886 --> 00:05:23,783
to share with us, "Creativity with AI:

122
00:05:23,783 --> 00:05:27,190
Imagining the Future of Student Success."

123
00:05:27,190 --> 00:05:32,190
(audience applauding) (cheerful music)

124
00:05:36,119 --> 00:05:37,589
- Hello, hello!

125
00:05:37,589 --> 00:05:39,180
It's so nice to be here with you all.

126
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:41,243
Thanks for hanging in on the afternoon,

127
00:05:41,243 --> 00:05:44,318
all that post-lunch digestion happening.

128
00:05:44,318 --> 00:05:45,840
It's great to be here.

129
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:47,850
I've loved spending time with so many of you

130
00:05:47,850 --> 00:05:49,560
and learning with you.

131
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:51,893
I'm here today to talk about creativity,

132
00:05:51,893 --> 00:05:53,790
the kind of creativity that went into

133
00:05:53,790 --> 00:05:56,250
that awesome short film that you just saw,

134
00:05:56,250 --> 00:05:59,630
which could have been an essay, but it was a film.

135
00:05:59,630 --> 00:06:01,320
It could have been done solo,

136
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:03,660
but it was done collaboratively as a team.

137
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:05,880
And there's so many dozens of other skills

138
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:08,190
that the creators learned along the ways,

139
00:06:08,190 --> 00:06:10,184
all that are hallmarks of creativity,

140
00:06:10,184 --> 00:06:11,790
by which I mean, creative thinking,

141
00:06:11,790 --> 00:06:15,387
creative problem solving, innovation, storytelling.

142
00:06:15,387 --> 00:06:17,700
And in that spirit, as I begin,

143
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:20,372
I wanted to share with you a little bit about my story

144
00:06:20,372 --> 00:06:23,143
and how education and creativity brought me

145
00:06:23,143 --> 00:06:26,111
from the classroom, to now my role at Adobe,

146
00:06:26,111 --> 00:06:27,450
which speaks a little bit

147
00:06:27,450 --> 00:06:28,860
to my own sense of purpose,

148
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:31,833
which is gonna be a key thing in my talk today.

149
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:35,809
So flashback about 25 years,

150
00:06:35,809 --> 00:06:37,755
I grew up in rural Montana,

151
00:06:37,755 --> 00:06:40,071
just outside my mom's reservation.

152
00:06:40,071 --> 00:06:42,174
I was the first person in my family to go to college,

153
00:06:42,174 --> 00:06:44,849
and my family was very lukewarm,

154
00:06:44,849 --> 00:06:47,910
to very resistant to me going to college, in fact.

155
00:06:47,910 --> 00:06:50,040
No one I knew went to college before.

156
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:52,440
My mom didn't actually graduate high school.

157
00:06:52,440 --> 00:06:54,330
And there's just a lot of the baggage

158
00:06:54,330 --> 00:06:56,070
that comes with first generation, right?

159
00:06:56,070 --> 00:06:58,499
That sense of how much is it gonna cost,

160
00:06:58,499 --> 00:06:59,980
is it worth it?

161
00:06:59,980 --> 00:07:02,850
And I was so lucky, because one of my foster parents

162
00:07:02,850 --> 00:07:05,277
in high school was actually my English teacher.

163
00:07:05,277 --> 00:07:07,740
And so, she of course forced me

164
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:09,818
to write the college application essay.

165
00:07:09,818 --> 00:07:13,170
But she also equipped me with something that made me feel

166
00:07:13,170 --> 00:07:15,020
like there's at least one thing I'll be able to do

167
00:07:15,020 --> 00:07:17,514
at college, which was the essay.

168
00:07:17,514 --> 00:07:19,290
By the time I graduated,

169
00:07:19,290 --> 00:07:20,937
I could write a five paragraph essay,

170
00:07:20,937 --> 00:07:22,796
to a five page essay.

171
00:07:22,796 --> 00:07:24,780
And she and the rest of my teachers said,

172
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:26,400
especially as an English major,

173
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:28,170
this is the one thing that you've got going for you.

174
00:07:28,170 --> 00:07:30,180
You've got this in the bag, you got the thesis statement,

175
00:07:30,180 --> 00:07:33,594
you got the MLA formatting, you're gonna be okay.

176
00:07:33,594 --> 00:07:35,430
Well, total bait and switch.

177
00:07:35,430 --> 00:07:38,760
I went to Montana State University, again, 25 years ago,

178
00:07:38,760 --> 00:07:40,710
and I don't know what was in the water there,

179
00:07:40,710 --> 00:07:44,190
but almost all of my faculty were having me do everything

180
00:07:44,190 --> 00:07:45,562
besides an essay.

181
00:07:45,562 --> 00:07:48,466
So I'd take a 19th century American literature course,

182
00:07:48,466 --> 00:07:50,817
and my professor would say, "All right, for your final,

183
00:07:50,817 --> 00:07:52,612
I want you to make a mix tape.

184
00:07:52,612 --> 00:07:54,418
Choose at least 12 songs,

185
00:07:54,418 --> 00:07:57,510
and write a paragraph about why something about that song

186
00:07:57,510 --> 00:08:00,570
or the lyrics tied to one of the readings of the course."

187
00:08:00,570 --> 00:08:03,143
Or they'd have us do plays or design posters,

188
00:08:03,143 --> 00:08:06,080
and I didn't know that this wasn't normal,

189
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:07,953
but two really cool things happened.

190
00:08:09,030 --> 00:08:11,580
One was I kind of put myself out there

191
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:13,950
and realized that I really like creating.

192
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:15,290
I like making things that didn't exist before.

193
00:08:15,290 --> 00:08:18,110
The other cool thing that was happening was all

194
00:08:18,110 --> 00:08:20,395
of these things I thought I had to hide, right?

195
00:08:20,395 --> 00:08:25,045
I mean, the laundry list, low income, rural, gay,

196
00:08:25,045 --> 00:08:28,470
first generation, mixed race.

197
00:08:28,470 --> 00:08:31,050
Like, all of these things that I thought were things

198
00:08:31,050 --> 00:08:32,940
that I wasn't really comfortable sharing.

199
00:08:32,940 --> 00:08:34,320
But when you do creative work,

200
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:36,243
it's kind of hard for those things not to show up.

201
00:08:36,243 --> 00:08:39,064
And so, I got to know my classmates a lot more.

202
00:08:39,064 --> 00:08:41,910
And I also got to know my faculty really, really well,

203
00:08:41,910 --> 00:08:43,440
and they got to know me.

204
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:46,650
And so, after a while, I was getting my degree

205
00:08:46,650 --> 00:08:49,518
in undergrad in K-12 teaching.

206
00:08:49,518 --> 00:08:51,608
A couple of my faculty said, "You know, Brian,

207
00:08:51,608 --> 00:08:53,997
you really like this college thing.

208
00:08:53,997 --> 00:08:55,643
Yes, you could be a K-12 teacher.

209
00:08:55,643 --> 00:08:58,020
I think you might want to teach college."

210
00:08:58,020 --> 00:08:59,670
And I loved medieval literature.

211
00:08:59,670 --> 00:09:01,992
So they were like, "Why don't you apply to grad school?"

212
00:09:01,992 --> 00:09:05,100
Which seemed as ridiculous to me as applying to undergrad.

213
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:06,390
But again, I had some teachers

214
00:09:06,390 --> 00:09:08,310
that were kind of forcing me to do it.

215
00:09:08,310 --> 00:09:09,911
And the really bizarre thing is,

216
00:09:09,911 --> 00:09:12,210
thank goodness none of us knew any better.

217
00:09:12,210 --> 00:09:14,197
In little Montana state 25 years ago, they're like,

218
00:09:14,197 --> 00:09:16,086
"Oh, well you like medieval literature.

219
00:09:16,086 --> 00:09:18,630
Oxford seems like it's like the birthplace

220
00:09:18,630 --> 00:09:19,644
of medieval literature.

221
00:09:19,644 --> 00:09:21,840
There's a scholarship called the Rhodes Scholarship.

222
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:22,860
Why don't you just apply to that,

223
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:24,643
and then, you can have money to go to Oxford?"

224
00:09:24,643 --> 00:09:27,948
Thank goodness none of us knew like how improbable that was.

225
00:09:27,948 --> 00:09:31,200
And then, right, I guess, like, I was very lucky.

226
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:32,370
I did get to go to Oxford.

227
00:09:32,370 --> 00:09:33,919
I got the Rhodes Scholarship.

228
00:09:33,919 --> 00:09:36,060
I went to Oxford for a couple years,

229
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:37,950
and the teaching was so different,

230
00:09:37,950 --> 00:09:39,420
but I thought, "This makes sense.

231
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:42,413
It's Oxford, it's literally a medieval institution."

232
00:09:42,413 --> 00:09:44,700
It was very essay focused.

233
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:47,332
No PDFs, no LMS, no, none of that.

234
00:09:47,332 --> 00:09:49,920
And then, I went on to do my PhD at Stanford.

235
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:51,660
And I was like, "All right, here we go.

236
00:09:51,660 --> 00:09:53,730
Stanford, Silicon Valley."

237
00:09:53,730 --> 00:09:55,320
Like, cutting edge technology.

238
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:56,550
And I had been teaching K-12,

239
00:09:56,550 --> 00:09:58,620
so I was really used to Edtech.

240
00:09:58,620 --> 00:10:01,032
And then, I was shocked when I went to Stanford,

241
00:10:01,032 --> 00:10:03,270
and it was chalk and talk. (audience chuckling)

242
00:10:03,270 --> 00:10:04,710
It was sage on the stage.

243
00:10:04,710 --> 00:10:07,080
And I was like, I mean, can I at least get a smart board,

244
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:08,070
like, let alone like, (audience laughing)

245
00:10:08,070 --> 00:10:10,689
a projector in a lot of my rooms?

246
00:10:10,689 --> 00:10:13,500
And so, what happened was towards the end of my PhD,

247
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:15,733
even though my PhD was not in education to begin with,

248
00:10:15,733 --> 00:10:17,862
I started doing more and more Edtech.

249
00:10:17,862 --> 00:10:19,620
And then, after getting my PhD,

250
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:21,240
I actually built a research lab

251
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:24,570
at Stanford focused on creativity.

252
00:10:24,570 --> 00:10:25,920
How can, at this time,

253
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:28,560
a lot of Edtech tools were very STEM focused,

254
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:29,761
very critical thinking.

255
00:10:29,761 --> 00:10:32,077
So I started building tools and saying,

256
00:10:32,077 --> 00:10:33,990
"If we let students engage with texts

257
00:10:33,990 --> 00:10:37,121
and their readings in creative ways, what can happen?'

258
00:10:37,121 --> 00:10:39,180
And I actually got to do a lot of co-teaching

259
00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:42,150
and piloting of those tools at Foothill-De Anza College,

260
00:10:42,150 --> 00:10:44,733
which was so much fun for me;

261
00:10:44,733 --> 00:10:48,427
all of which is to say what a circuitous route

262
00:10:48,427 --> 00:10:50,213
to a few years later,

263
00:10:50,213 --> 00:10:53,010
Adobe had a role and they said, "You know what?

264
00:10:53,010 --> 00:10:55,252
