526
00:21:39,690 --> 00:21:41,449
we're actually getting more of this in the classroom,

527
00:21:41,449 --> 00:21:43,713
so we can see more of these benefits?

528
00:21:45,492 --> 00:21:48,660
But of course, student success is holistic.

529
00:21:48,660 --> 00:21:50,562
And beyond the classroom,

530
00:21:50,562 --> 00:21:52,980
as educators, we're also stewards

531
00:21:52,980 --> 00:21:55,487
for ensuring that students have full equitable access

532
00:21:55,487 --> 00:21:58,337
to career opportunities based on the skills

533
00:21:58,337 --> 00:22:00,960
that they're acquiring in our classes.

534
00:22:00,960 --> 00:22:03,150
And when it comes to essential skills for every job

535
00:22:03,150 --> 00:22:06,013
and industry, creativity is top of the list.

536
00:22:06,013 --> 00:22:08,160
Quite literally, top of the list.

537
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:11,291
What you're looking at here is a list of career skills

538
00:22:11,291 --> 00:22:14,546
that are rising in significance in the next two years,

539
00:22:14,546 --> 00:22:17,250
according to a global cross industry study

540
00:22:17,250 --> 00:22:19,119
by the World Economic Forum.

541
00:22:19,119 --> 00:22:20,991
They do this every two years.

542
00:22:20,991 --> 00:22:23,611
Creativity has stayed one, two, and three,

543
00:22:23,611 --> 00:22:26,121
basically, for the past couple decades,

544
00:22:26,121 --> 00:22:28,920
which is why, about five years ago,

545
00:22:28,920 --> 00:22:30,840
the World Economic Forum created

546
00:22:30,840 --> 00:22:32,940
their first education initiative.

547
00:22:32,940 --> 00:22:35,130
And it focused primarily on K-12.

548
00:22:35,130 --> 00:22:37,405
And the reason that they built this is,

549
00:22:37,405 --> 00:22:39,840
the World Economic Forum really cares

550
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:40,954
about supporting industries

551
00:22:40,954 --> 00:22:42,657
with the skills that they need.

552
00:22:42,657 --> 00:22:44,497
And this was the first time they said,

553
00:22:44,497 --> 00:22:46,500
"We can't do industry training alone.

554
00:22:46,500 --> 00:22:49,435
The shift is so big, we have to start thinking K-12

555
00:22:49,435 --> 00:22:51,450
and higher ed institutions.

556
00:22:51,450 --> 00:22:53,221
How can we make sure that they're getting the skills

557
00:22:53,221 --> 00:22:55,500
that are needed for the workforce?"

558
00:22:55,500 --> 00:22:57,120
So they created a board of about 20 people,

559
00:22:57,120 --> 00:23:00,634
invited Adobe, because our company founded on creativity,

560
00:23:00,634 --> 00:23:03,033
and this sort of needed to be solved.

561
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:08,790
Of course, AI has sort of changed everything.

562
00:23:08,790 --> 00:23:11,250
This is also from the World Economic Forum,

563
00:23:11,250 --> 00:23:12,704
pretty staggering numbers.

564
00:23:12,704 --> 00:23:15,001
They estimate that in the next five years,

565
00:23:15,001 --> 00:23:17,700
there's gonna be another 92 million jobs

566
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:20,250
that are displaced because of AI.

567
00:23:20,250 --> 00:23:23,394
Not great, but there's this other opportunity here.

568
00:23:23,394 --> 00:23:26,820
They also estimate that there's gonna be 170 million new

569
00:23:26,820 --> 00:23:28,113
and emerging jobs.

570
00:23:28,950 --> 00:23:31,269
So how are employers navigating that shift

571
00:23:31,269 --> 00:23:35,010
to make sure that workers are either skilled, or re-skilled,

572
00:23:35,010 --> 00:23:36,569
or upskilled for the shift,

573
00:23:36,569 --> 00:23:38,550
so that we actually create opportunity,

574
00:23:38,550 --> 00:23:39,903
not losing opportunity?

575
00:23:41,411 --> 00:23:43,734
These are the top strategies

576
00:23:43,734 --> 00:23:47,790
that employers across industries, globally, are using.

577
00:23:47,790 --> 00:23:48,847
Eighty-five percent said,

578
00:23:48,847 --> 00:23:50,280
"Well, we have a lot of employees today.

579
00:23:50,280 --> 00:23:51,300
We need to upskill them.

580
00:23:51,300 --> 00:23:52,738
We're gonna train them all on AI

581
00:23:52,738 --> 00:23:55,181
and the essential skills that we need."

582
00:23:55,181 --> 00:23:56,846
Seventy-three percent said

583
00:23:56,846 --> 00:23:58,740
that they're working on automation

584
00:23:58,740 --> 00:24:00,369
of processes and tasks.

585
00:24:00,369 --> 00:24:02,296
Seventy percent said hiring.

586
00:24:02,296 --> 00:24:03,597
This is the only way we're gonna get

587
00:24:03,597 --> 00:24:05,130
the skill builder company.

588
00:24:05,130 --> 00:24:07,020
We need to find people that already have them

589
00:24:07,020 --> 00:24:09,420
so we can hire them, which is a lot easier,

590
00:24:09,420 --> 00:24:12,093
sometimes, than re-skilling an existing workforce.

591
00:24:14,430 --> 00:24:18,714
This is from the 2024 Work Trend Annual Index Report,

592
00:24:18,714 --> 00:24:21,540
where they ask leaders, again, across industries,

593
00:24:21,540 --> 00:24:24,286
how AI is affecting their hiring processes.

594
00:24:24,286 --> 00:24:26,179
Seventy-one percent of leaders

595
00:24:26,179 --> 00:24:29,640
across these industries are now more likely

596
00:24:29,640 --> 00:24:32,780
to hire a less experienced candidate with AI skills

597
00:24:32,780 --> 00:24:34,923
than a more experienced one without.

598
00:24:36,054 --> 00:24:38,355
To me, this is a very exciting,

599
00:24:38,355 --> 00:24:41,217
I think, very new moment in history,

600
00:24:41,217 --> 00:24:43,615
filled with opportunity for our students.

601
00:24:43,615 --> 00:24:46,117
Because now, they have a chance to say in an interview,

602
00:24:46,117 --> 00:24:47,010
"You know what?

603
00:24:47,010 --> 00:24:48,840
I don't have five years of experience as a marketer.

604
00:24:48,840 --> 00:24:52,260
I don't have two years of experience as a project manager.

605
00:24:52,260 --> 00:24:55,311
I don't have seven years of experience as a grant writer.

606
00:24:55,311 --> 00:24:58,329
But what I do have is the ability to use AI

607
00:24:58,329 --> 00:25:00,959
to be more efficient, more innovative,

608
00:25:00,959 --> 00:25:03,503
and to help your broader team learn

609
00:25:03,503 --> 00:25:05,640
how we can get the work done

610
00:25:05,640 --> 00:25:07,240
and get better results with AI."

611
00:25:09,390 --> 00:25:11,340
Seventy-seven percent of leaders say early

612
00:25:11,340 --> 00:25:14,070
in career talent is gonna get greater responsibilities

613
00:25:14,070 --> 00:25:15,390
due to AI.

614
00:25:15,390 --> 00:25:17,024
And I say thank goodness,

615
00:25:17,024 --> 00:25:20,168
because there is so much uninspiring, grunt work

616
00:25:20,168 --> 00:25:23,310
the first few jobs or first few years on your jobs.

617
00:25:23,310 --> 00:25:25,666
So sort of menial or repetitive tasks

618
00:25:25,666 --> 00:25:29,036
that are not particularly exciting or passionate.

619
00:25:29,036 --> 00:25:31,320
Employees are saying, "Well, if you have AI,

620
00:25:31,320 --> 00:25:32,635
we're gonna give you bigger jobs,

621
00:25:32,635 --> 00:25:34,479
whole projects that you can work on,

622
00:25:34,479 --> 00:25:36,900
because you can now uplevel some of your skills

623
00:25:36,900 --> 00:25:39,125
that are gonna be needed."

624
00:25:39,125 --> 00:25:41,476
And finally, perhaps, the most sobering is 66%

625
00:25:41,476 --> 00:25:43,822
of leaders say they would not hire someone

626
00:25:43,822 --> 00:25:45,443
without AI skills.

627
00:25:45,443 --> 00:25:47,340
Now, this report's almost a year old,

628
00:25:47,340 --> 00:25:50,433
so I imagine this number has certainly gone up since then.

629
00:25:51,561 --> 00:25:53,139
With all of this in mind,

630
00:25:53,139 --> 00:25:54,960
how does creativity come (notification chiming)

631
00:25:54,960 --> 00:25:55,793
into play?

632
00:25:56,820 --> 00:26:00,486
Creatives have gotten this memo more than anyone.

633
00:26:00,486 --> 00:26:03,321
These are the occupations that have had the largest increase

634
00:26:03,321 --> 00:26:06,540
of members on LinkedIn adding AI skills

635
00:26:06,540 --> 00:26:08,291
to their profiles in the past year.

636
00:26:08,291 --> 00:26:11,262
And you can see in the sort of rainbow spectrum here,

637
00:26:11,262 --> 00:26:14,280
creative roles, content writer, graphic designer,

638
00:26:14,280 --> 00:26:17,006
product designer, you can even argue marketing manager,

639
00:26:17,006 --> 00:26:17,839
front end developer.

640
00:26:17,839 --> 00:26:19,668
Those are also very creative roles.

641
00:26:19,668 --> 00:26:22,730
They're at the top of the pack in upskilling

642
00:26:22,730 --> 00:26:25,170
in response to a huge industry demand

643
00:26:25,170 --> 00:26:27,970
for these new skills, so that they can stay competitive.

644
00:26:29,029 --> 00:26:30,450
So this motivation,

645
00:26:30,450 --> 00:26:35,340
helping students to unlock and acquire those durable skills

646
00:26:35,340 --> 00:26:37,236
to have those career opportunities was

647
00:26:37,236 --> 00:26:39,390
also a really strong motivator

648
00:26:39,390 --> 00:26:41,788
for the faculty that participated in this study.

649
00:26:41,788 --> 00:26:44,820
Eighty-six percent of educators believe teaching students

650
00:26:44,820 --> 00:26:47,367
these creative generative AI skills is gonna enhance

651
00:26:47,367 --> 00:26:50,739
their chances of getting a job, and not just any jobs,

652
00:26:50,739 --> 00:26:53,100
but jobs that they're actually passionate about.

653
00:26:53,100 --> 00:26:54,690
Very important.

654
00:26:54,690 --> 00:26:58,050
And 38% reported that improved student career prospects is

655
00:26:58,050 --> 00:27:00,870
among their top reasons for encouraging the use

656
00:27:00,870 --> 00:27:01,983
of responsible AI.

657
00:27:03,160 --> 00:27:07,691
Let me give you an example of that piece about passion

658
00:27:07,691 --> 00:27:10,274
and why I think it's important.

659
00:27:11,423 --> 00:27:14,820
This is a quote from a business faculty member in Georgia,

660
00:27:14,820 --> 00:27:18,420
who shared with me that, "AI can significantly contribute

661
00:27:18,420 --> 00:27:20,859
to student well-being and a sense of purpose

662
00:27:20,859 --> 00:27:23,999
by enhancing their creativity and self-expression

663
00:27:23,999 --> 00:27:25,354
in various ways.

664
00:27:25,354 --> 00:27:27,420
It can help students express themselves,

665
00:27:27,420 --> 00:27:29,190
build confidence with creative wins,

666
00:27:29,190 --> 00:27:31,309
explore identity, reduce stress.

667
00:27:31,309 --> 00:27:33,930
Whether it's offering a safe space for self-expression

668
00:27:33,930 --> 00:27:35,346
or helping them connect with others,

669
00:27:35,346 --> 00:27:38,907
AI can be a powerful ally in supporting student well-being."

670
00:27:39,844 --> 00:27:43,140
Now, well-being, especially purpose,

671
00:27:43,140 --> 00:27:46,053
came up so frequently in my pre-research for this study

672
00:27:46,053 --> 00:27:48,780
that I couldn't help pull on that thread a little bit.

673
00:27:48,780 --> 00:27:52,260
And I got really curious about the role that it plays here.

674
00:27:52,260 --> 00:27:55,830
So I had the opportunity to spend half a day

675
00:27:55,830 --> 00:27:59,250
with kind of the world's expert on purpose.

676
00:27:59,250 --> 00:28:00,120
His name's Vic Strecher,

677
00:28:00,120 --> 00:28:02,370
he's a professor at the University of Michigan.

678
00:28:02,370 --> 00:28:05,010
He's sectioned the School of Public Health,

679
00:28:05,010 --> 00:28:07,770
and he's literally written the book, "On Purpose,"

680
00:28:07,770 --> 00:28:10,189
and it's one of the the top performing Coursera courses

681
00:28:10,189 --> 00:28:11,893
on the power of purpose.

682
00:28:11,893 --> 00:28:15,390
So there's a lot of research on how purpose-driven companies

683
00:28:15,390 --> 00:28:16,740
and employees with a high sense

684
00:28:16,740 --> 00:28:19,482
of purpose generally do better in the workplace.

685
00:28:19,482 --> 00:28:21,261
But as an expert in public health,

686
00:28:21,261 --> 00:28:23,297
Vic became particularly interested,

687
00:28:23,297 --> 00:28:26,400
because when you use this empirical measurement

688
00:28:26,400 --> 00:28:28,050
of how high someone's sense of purpose is,

689
00:28:28,050 --> 00:28:30,935
health outcomes are actually highly correlated.

690
00:28:30,935 --> 00:28:33,030
So people with a strong sense of purpose,

691
00:28:33,030 --> 00:28:35,040
they have better immune systems, lower stress,

692
00:28:35,040 --> 00:28:37,800
better cognitive function, decreased risk of heart disease,

693
00:28:37,800 --> 00:28:39,995
live longer, and a lot more.

694
00:28:39,995 --> 00:28:43,970
And as Vic shared, he sort of breaks up the seven main ways

695
00:28:43,970 --> 00:28:46,250
in which people will have a sense of purpose

696
00:28:46,250 --> 00:28:48,028
that kind of fill their buckets.

697
00:28:48,028 --> 00:28:49,889
Creativity is one of those main ways.

698
00:28:49,889 --> 00:28:51,840
People say, "I find a sense of purpose

699
00:28:51,840 --> 00:28:54,957
by trying new things, brainstorming new ways to do them,

700
00:28:54,957 --> 00:28:58,164
bringing something to life that just didn't exist before."

701
00:28:58,164 --> 00:29:00,300
And that sort of buzz that you get,

702
00:29:00,300 --> 00:29:02,750
because it feels great, because it came from you.

703
00:29:03,930 --> 00:29:05,670
So because there's this empirical measure,

704
00:29:05,670 --> 00:29:07,594
we can ask someone how high their sense our purpose is,

705
00:29:07,594 --> 00:29:10,346
I couldn't help but use that in this study as well,

706
00:29:10,346 --> 00:29:11,760
because I got curious.

707
00:29:11,760 --> 00:29:14,070
So just like the cohorts of educators,

708
00:29:14,070 --> 00:29:18,207
the top 25 and bottom 25th percentile for creativity,

709
00:29:18,207 --> 00:29:21,370
I broke them into several buckets around purpose,

710
00:29:21,370 --> 00:29:23,007
the lowest sense of purpose

711
00:29:23,007 --> 00:29:24,942
and the highest sense of purpose educators.

712
00:29:24,942 --> 00:29:26,580
Because education, after all,

713
00:29:26,580 --> 00:29:28,950
is a very purpose-driven profession.

714
00:29:28,950 --> 00:29:30,060
And I wanted to get a sense of,

715
00:29:30,060 --> 00:29:31,950
those educators with a higher low sense of purpose,

716
00:29:31,950 --> 00:29:34,400
how much is that translating over their students?

717
00:29:37,290 --> 00:29:40,807
So what you're seeing here is I ask them,

718
00:29:40,807 --> 00:29:42,480
whose responsibility is it

719
00:29:42,480 --> 00:29:45,510
that students have a sense of their purpose

720
00:29:45,510 --> 00:29:47,150
before graduating?

721
00:29:47,150 --> 00:29:50,400
Faculty in the US with a low sense of purpose,

722
00:29:50,400 --> 00:29:53,042
so this is the bottom 25th percentile,

723
00:29:53,042 --> 00:29:54,170
it's pretty split.

724
00:29:54,170 --> 00:29:56,430
For students figuring out what they want to do

725
00:29:56,430 --> 00:29:57,270
with their lives,

726
00:29:57,270 --> 00:30:00,560
29% say that's the student's responsibility, not my job.

727
00:30:00,560 --> 00:30:02,670
Forty-three percent said a little bit of me,

728
00:30:02,670 --> 00:30:03,549
a little bit of them.

729
00:30:03,549 --> 00:30:06,300
Twenty-eight said it's my job as an instructor

730
00:30:06,300 --> 00:30:08,820
to make sure the students leave here graduating,

731
00:30:08,820 --> 00:30:10,383
knowing their sense of purpose.

732
00:30:12,450 --> 00:30:14,040
Let's look to the right educators

733
00:30:14,040 --> 00:30:16,503
in the top 25th percentile of purpose.

734
00:30:17,640 --> 00:30:20,790
Only 14% are fully passing the buck onto their students,

735
00:30:20,790 --> 00:30:22,832
and a full 88%, if you combine them,

736
00:30:22,832 --> 00:30:26,640
of those high purpose educators are taking ownership

737
00:30:26,640 --> 00:30:28,020
for ensuring that their students

738
00:30:28,020 --> 00:30:30,270
also have a sense of purpose in their life.

739
00:30:30,270 --> 00:30:31,103
They're the ones

740
00:30:31,103 --> 00:30:33,330
where students aren't just learning long division,

741
00:30:33,330 --> 00:30:34,230
they aren't just learning

742
00:30:34,230 --> 00:30:36,270
that mitochondria is the powerhouse of the cell,

743
00:30:36,270 --> 00:30:38,250
all that important content knowledge.

744
00:30:38,250 --> 00:30:39,780
But they're also expressing themselves

745
00:30:39,780 --> 00:30:41,550
and figuring out what lights them up,

746
00:30:41,550 --> 00:30:43,489
what gets a fire in their belly.

747
00:30:43,489 --> 00:30:45,959
And I think this kind of creative exploration

748
00:30:45,959 --> 00:30:48,061
and opportunity that we can give students,

749
00:30:48,061 --> 00:30:50,580
for me, is probably one of the most exciting ways

750
00:30:50,580 --> 00:30:53,139
in which we can be using AI in the classroom today,

751
00:30:53,139 --> 00:30:54,580
because a lots at stake,

752
00:30:54,580 --> 00:30:58,140
and the next generation needs purpose more than ever.

753
00:30:58,140 --> 00:31:01,890
In primary school, 80% of students don't have a clear idea

754
00:31:01,890 --> 00:31:04,260
of their sense of purpose, which is a shame,

755
00:31:04,260 --> 00:31:05,250
because research has shown

756
00:31:05,250 --> 00:31:07,920
that those who do tend to have better mental health

757
00:31:07,920 --> 00:31:10,066
and more resilience against setbacks.

758
00:31:10,066 --> 00:31:12,648
By high school, things don't improve much.

759
00:31:12,648 --> 00:31:14,490
Fifty-eight percent of young adults

760
00:31:14,490 --> 00:31:16,860
in the US say they lack any meaning or purpose

761
00:31:16,860 --> 00:31:18,060
in their lives.

762
00:31:18,060 --> 00:31:18,930
And half report

763
00:31:18,930 --> 00:31:21,180
that their mental health is negatively influenced by that.

764
00:31:21,180 --> 00:31:24,298
And for those who say, "Well, that's what college is for,"

765
00:31:24,298 --> 00:31:26,939
to figure out what you want to do with your life,

766
00:31:26,939 --> 00:31:30,442
95% of college graduates consider a sense

767
00:31:30,442 --> 00:31:32,731
of purpose important in their work,

768
00:31:32,731 --> 00:31:35,340
but only 40% of recent graduates say

769
00:31:35,340 --> 00:31:37,700
that they've actually found a career that feels meaningful

770
00:31:37,700 --> 00:31:39,998
or a job that feels meaningful to them.

771
00:31:39,998 --> 00:31:42,730
Because if you don't know what your sense of purpose is,

772
00:31:42,730 --> 00:31:45,270
then, you can't make purpose driven decisions

773
00:31:45,270 --> 00:31:46,470
about your life.

774
00:31:46,470 --> 00:31:48,900
If I don't know that being creative lights me up,

775
00:31:48,900 --> 00:31:51,330
I don't know to look for, to acquire that skill

776
00:31:51,330 --> 00:31:53,591
and to look for those jobs.

777
00:31:53,591 --> 00:31:58,080
So I'll end by saying, this study really highlighted,

778
00:31:58,080 --> 00:32:01,824
for me, that how holistic student success really is.

779
00:32:01,824 --> 00:32:04,946
That academic achievement, career success,

780
00:32:04,946 --> 00:32:06,727
and personal well-being

781
00:32:06,727 --> 00:32:08,760
and that sort of sense of purpose,

782
00:32:08,760 --> 00:32:10,050
these are all intertwined,

783
00:32:10,050 --> 00:32:13,020
which I think we kind of just know at a gut level.

784
00:32:13,020 --> 00:32:15,990
But what also showed is creativity connects these gaps,

785
00:32:15,990 --> 00:32:18,150
and the more creativity and creative thinking

786
00:32:18,150 --> 00:32:19,921
that we can bring to our students,

787
00:32:19,921 --> 00:32:22,650
the more it really raises all votes.

788
00:32:22,650 --> 00:32:25,590
So that's why I'm excited about AI today.

789
00:32:25,590 --> 00:32:28,962
I'm the first (indistinct) that is an AI optimist,

