1280
00:56:27,030 --> 00:56:30,330
It's 1594, and there's a Dutch farmer.

1281
00:56:30,330 --> 00:56:31,800
I'm originally from the Netherlands.

1282
00:56:31,800 --> 00:56:36,800
And there's a Dutch farmer, and he invents a sawmill.

1283
00:56:37,226 --> 00:56:39,404
And this thing is a, it's a windmill,

1284
00:56:39,404 --> 00:56:43,620
and it's got like 16 blades that go up and down.

1285
00:56:43,620 --> 00:56:45,870
And it drags the log from the canal

1286
00:56:45,870 --> 00:56:48,480
into these 16 blades and cuts,

1287
00:56:48,480 --> 00:56:53,480
16 blades cut a log into 17 planks.

1288
00:56:53,654 --> 00:56:55,530
That's his idea.

1289
00:56:55,530 --> 00:56:58,702
So he goes to the Sawyer's guild in Amsterdam,

1290
00:56:58,702 --> 00:57:00,210
and he says, "I got this great,

1291
00:57:00,210 --> 00:57:02,880
new labor-saving device invention."

1292
00:57:02,880 --> 00:57:05,250
And he demonstrates it to them.

1293
00:57:05,250 --> 00:57:08,370
And they said, "There is no effing way we're gonna let

1294
00:57:08,370 --> 00:57:11,822
this thing anywhere near anywhere where we work,

1295
00:57:11,822 --> 00:57:14,727
because we're all gonna lose our jobs."

1296
00:57:16,033 --> 00:57:16,920
Okay?

1297
00:57:16,920 --> 00:57:20,855
So he said, "Okay, you don't want it, that's fine."

1298
00:57:20,855 --> 00:57:22,920
He goes, and he does it anyway.

1299
00:57:22,920 --> 00:57:26,730
He does it, you know, a couple of miles from Amsterdam.

1300
00:57:26,730 --> 00:57:28,290
He builds it.

1301
00:57:28,290 --> 00:57:33,290
Holland went from being the smallest,

1302
00:57:33,617 --> 00:57:37,500
how can I say, maritime country in Europe, to the biggest.

1303
00:57:37,500 --> 00:57:38,795
How do they do that?

1304
00:57:38,795 --> 00:57:43,163
When they started log, creating those planks,

1305
00:57:43,163 --> 00:57:45,478
they improved the efficiency

1306
00:57:45,478 --> 00:57:49,144
of creating planks by 3000%.

1307
00:57:49,144 --> 00:57:52,150
Instead of doing something that took four months,

1308
00:57:52,150 --> 00:57:53,968
it would now take a week.

1309
00:57:53,968 --> 00:57:58,359
Holland out planked England and France put together

1310
00:57:58,359 --> 00:58:02,280
and became the greatest maritime nation in Europe.

1311
00:58:02,280 --> 00:58:05,989
Beat the Spanish in a war on the ocean,

1312
00:58:05,989 --> 00:58:08,643
and the same thing, beat the Brits.

1313
00:58:09,597 --> 00:58:14,527
So all those people who were sawing planks

1314
00:58:14,527 --> 00:58:19,083
before by hand end up building ships.

1315
00:58:19,083 --> 00:58:21,810
Now, there's a moral to the story, of course.

1316
00:58:21,810 --> 00:58:22,950
I mean, like, you think

1317
00:58:22,950 --> 00:58:25,530
that a labor saving device is gonna destroy your job.

1318
00:58:25,530 --> 00:58:26,430
No, it's not.

1319
00:58:26,430 --> 00:58:28,290
It's gonna create other jobs.

1320
00:58:28,290 --> 00:58:30,210
1934 is the other one.

1321
00:58:30,210 --> 00:58:33,440
1934, there was a strike,

1322
00:58:33,440 --> 00:58:37,467
the long dock worker strike in San Francisco.

1323
00:58:37,467 --> 00:58:41,486
Two people died, a couple hundred people were injured.

1324
00:58:41,486 --> 00:58:43,998
It was a major, major strike.

1325
00:58:43,998 --> 00:58:48,660
And so, it changed labor laws, and so on, and so forth.

1326
00:58:48,660 --> 00:58:51,783
But today, how many dock workers do we have?

1327
00:58:52,920 --> 00:58:55,340
We have containers, so there's no more dock workers.

1328
00:58:55,340 --> 00:58:57,660
So what did all those people do?

1329
00:58:57,660 --> 00:59:00,540
They drive trucks, or they have other jobs.

1330
00:59:00,540 --> 00:59:01,890
Anyway, AI is like that.

1331
00:59:01,890 --> 00:59:04,143
It's coming along and it's going to,

1332
00:59:04,143 --> 00:59:06,802
yes, it's gonna take away jobs that are,

1333
00:59:06,802 --> 00:59:10,380
in my humble opinion, not really jobs.

1334
00:59:10,380 --> 00:59:13,959
You know, if a machine can do what I do,

1335
00:59:13,959 --> 00:59:17,494
it's not a job, it's, a machine should do what I do.

1336
00:59:17,494 --> 00:59:19,609
That's just the way I think about it.

1337
00:59:19,609 --> 00:59:22,113
My name is Henk Rogers.

1338
00:59:22,113 --> 00:59:24,040
I majored in computer science

1339
00:59:24,040 --> 00:59:26,026
and minored in Dungeons and Dragons

1340
00:59:26,026 --> 00:59:28,990
(audience laughing) at the University of Hawaii

1341
00:59:30,510 --> 00:59:32,220
in the late seventies.

1342
00:59:32,220 --> 00:59:35,072
This is before personal computers.

1343
00:59:35,072 --> 00:59:39,595
I moved to Japan, and six years later, in 1983,

1344
00:59:39,595 --> 00:59:42,299
when personal computers came out in Japan,

1345
00:59:42,299 --> 00:59:43,503
these were not IBMs,

1346
00:59:43,503 --> 00:59:47,100
they were like Japanese NECs from Japan's Sharp.

1347
00:59:47,100 --> 00:59:49,563
They all made their own computers back then.

1348
00:59:50,886 --> 00:59:53,160
I decided I'm gonna write a,

1349
00:59:53,160 --> 00:59:55,260
create a computer game.

1350
00:59:55,260 --> 00:59:57,813
So is anybody here coding?

1351
00:59:59,003 --> 01:00:00,150
Yeah, okay.

1352
01:00:00,150 --> 01:00:02,840
I wrote the first role playing game in Japan in 64K.

1353
01:00:02,840 --> 01:00:04,770
- [Audience Member] Wow. (audience laughing)

1354
01:00:04,770 --> 01:00:06,300
- That includes graphics,

1355
01:00:06,300 --> 01:00:08,130
it includes the story, (Henk chuckling)

1356
01:00:08,130 --> 01:00:08,963
it includes everything.

1357
01:00:08,963 --> 01:00:10,855
Who needs more than 64K, anyway?

1358
01:00:10,855 --> 01:00:12,753
(audience laughing)

1359
01:00:12,753 --> 01:00:15,840
So it was the number one game in Japan in 1984.

1360
01:00:15,840 --> 01:00:18,854
It launched my career as a publisher.

1361
01:00:18,854 --> 01:00:21,130
I wrote the first two games for the company.

1362
01:00:21,130 --> 01:00:22,830
Then, I traveled around the world looking

1363
01:00:22,830 --> 01:00:25,050
for games to bring to Japan.

1364
01:00:25,050 --> 01:00:30,050
And at a consumer electronic show in Las Vegas in 1988,

1365
01:00:30,367 --> 01:00:32,640
I found a little game called Tetris.

1366
01:00:32,640 --> 01:00:34,066
Now, how many of you played Tetris?

1367
01:00:34,066 --> 01:00:35,290
(audience muttering)

1368
01:00:35,290 --> 01:00:38,343
Oh, I'm so sorry, or... (audience laughing)

1369
01:00:38,343 --> 01:00:42,060
I don't know which way that one should go.

1370
01:00:42,060 --> 01:00:45,467
But I published Tetris in Japan

1371
01:00:45,467 --> 01:00:48,480
on seven different personal computer platforms,

1372
01:00:48,480 --> 01:00:50,160
none of which were compatible,

1373
01:00:50,160 --> 01:00:52,200
and on the Nintendo family computer,

1374
01:00:52,200 --> 01:00:55,380
which is the NES machine in the US.

1375
01:00:55,380 --> 01:00:59,985
And that year, Nintendo came out with Gameboy.

1376
01:00:59,985 --> 01:01:01,650
(audience exclaiming)

1377
01:01:01,650 --> 01:01:04,800
And in 1989, they were going to come out

1378
01:01:04,800 --> 01:01:07,980
with Gameboy in the US and the rest of the world.

1379
01:01:07,980 --> 01:01:09,480
And Nintendo had a policy

1380
01:01:09,480 --> 01:01:14,480
of including a game every time they release a new platform,

1381
01:01:14,520 --> 01:01:17,070
because they don't want somebody to buy a machine

1382
01:01:17,070 --> 01:01:19,245
and not be able to play a game right away.

1383
01:01:19,245 --> 01:01:21,810
And I thought Tetris would be the perfect game

1384
01:01:21,810 --> 01:01:23,190
for Gameboy.

1385
01:01:23,190 --> 01:01:25,740
And it turns out that it was.

1386
01:01:25,740 --> 01:01:27,690
So I went to meet with Mr. Arakawa,

1387
01:01:27,690 --> 01:01:29,437
the President of Nintendo of America, and I said,

1388
01:01:29,437 --> 01:01:31,395
"Mr. Arakawa,

1389
01:01:31,395 --> 01:01:34,574
I think I have the perfect game for Gameboy.

1390
01:01:34,574 --> 01:01:36,840
You should include this, you know, Tetris,

1391
01:01:36,840 --> 01:01:38,970
you should include it with every Gameboy."

1392
01:01:38,970 --> 01:01:42,300
And he said, "Why should I include Tetris?

1393
01:01:42,300 --> 01:01:43,579
I have Mario."

1394
01:01:43,579 --> 01:01:44,730
(audience chuckling)

1395
01:01:44,730 --> 01:01:47,220
And I said, "If you want little boys to buy your Gameboy,

1396
01:01:47,220 --> 01:01:48,120
include Mario.

1397
01:01:48,120 --> 01:01:52,080
If you want everyone to buy your Gameboy, include Tetris."

1398
01:01:52,080 --> 01:01:53,340
And he called in his experts,

1399
01:01:53,340 --> 01:01:54,930
he called in his experts, and they agreed.

1400
01:01:54,930 --> 01:01:57,030
And so, we made a deal, we shook hands,

1401
01:01:57,030 --> 01:02:00,690
and I didn't have the rights for Tetris for Gameboy.

1402
01:02:00,690 --> 01:02:01,969
Nobody did.

1403
01:02:01,969 --> 01:02:04,348
So in February of 1989,

1404
01:02:04,348 --> 01:02:07,020
I got on a plane with the tourist visa

1405
01:02:07,020 --> 01:02:08,435
to go to the Soviet Union

1406
01:02:08,435 --> 01:02:12,839
to find who had the rights to Tetris, which organization.

1407
01:02:12,839 --> 01:02:14,786
If you want to know that story,

1408
01:02:14,786 --> 01:02:16,476
there's a movie on Apple TV, have any of you've seen that?

1409
01:02:16,476 --> 01:02:19,225
- [Audience Member] Yeah. - There's a movie on Apple-

1410
01:02:19,225 --> 01:02:20,058
- [Audience Member] Oh.

1411
01:02:20,058 --> 01:02:23,137
- TV called, "Tetris." - [Audience Member] Yeah.

1412
01:02:23,137 --> 01:02:27,600
- The guy who played Elton John in "Rocketman" plays me,

1413
01:02:27,600 --> 01:02:30,412
or the... (audience laughing)

1414
01:02:30,412 --> 01:02:32,820
So his name is Taron Egerton.

1415
01:02:32,820 --> 01:02:34,868
He's a up and coming actor.

1416
01:02:34,868 --> 01:02:36,330
Very good actor, actually.

1417
01:02:36,330 --> 01:02:38,332
He looks nothing like me. (audience laughing)

1418
01:02:38,332 --> 01:02:40,320
He looks nothing like me, but he's a great actor.

1419
01:02:40,320 --> 01:02:42,690
So it was, you know, and I went through the script,

1420
01:02:42,690 --> 01:02:44,380
and it was kind of terrifying,

1421
01:02:44,380 --> 01:02:47,458
'cause the script had so much Hollywood nonsense in it.

1422
01:02:47,458 --> 01:02:49,441
(audience chuckling)

1423
01:02:49,441 --> 01:02:50,804
Car chase?

1424
01:02:50,804 --> 01:02:52,762
(audience laughing)

1425
01:02:52,762 --> 01:02:53,943
Seriously?

1426
01:02:54,953 --> 01:02:57,105
So anyway, I thought,

1427
01:02:57,105 --> 01:02:59,464
"Oh, this is gonna be such a man-made movie."

1428
01:02:59,464 --> 01:03:00,600
And so, I was,

1429
01:03:00,600 --> 01:03:03,810
I just sort of like shivered.

1430
01:03:03,810 --> 01:03:06,005
And when I went to watch the movie

1431
01:03:06,005 --> 01:03:10,153
for the first time, and it was actually a movie.

1432
01:03:10,153 --> 01:03:11,910
I actually cried several times

1433
01:03:11,910 --> 01:03:13,431
in that movie about things that never happened.

1434
01:03:13,431 --> 01:03:16,431
(audience laughing)

1435
01:03:17,443 --> 01:03:18,276
You know, me not showing up for my daughter's performance,

1436
01:03:21,240 --> 01:03:22,702
and et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

1437
01:03:22,702 --> 01:03:25,398
- [Audience Member] Oh. - All made up stuff.

1438
01:03:25,398 --> 01:03:27,690
And if you want to know the truth about

1439
01:03:27,690 --> 01:03:29,070
what actually happened back then,

1440
01:03:29,070 --> 01:03:32,808
I released a book on April Fool's Day this year.

1441
01:03:32,808 --> 01:03:35,103
It was called, "The Perfect Game..."

1442
01:03:35,103 --> 01:03:38,790
subtitled, "Tetris: from Russia With Love."

1443
01:03:38,790 --> 01:03:43,777
And so, it's my first foray into writing a book.

1444
01:03:43,777 --> 01:03:47,216
And actually, if you want to wait for the audio book,

1445
01:03:47,216 --> 01:03:49,062
I've just finished doing that too.

1446
01:03:49,062 --> 01:03:52,780
I spent four days in the studio reading my own book.

1447
01:03:52,780 --> 01:03:54,300
(audience laughing)

1448
01:03:54,300 --> 01:03:59,300
So fast forward, it's 2005,

1449
01:03:59,605 --> 01:04:02,149
and I just sold a company.

1450
01:04:02,149 --> 01:04:03,960
And the company was a company

1451
01:04:03,960 --> 01:04:06,120
that makes games for mobile phones.

1452
01:04:06,120 --> 01:04:09,030
We had, Tetris is our big game.

1453
01:04:09,030 --> 01:04:10,975
And of course, we had the number one game

1454
01:04:10,975 --> 01:04:13,689
in mobile phones at that time.

1455
01:04:13,689 --> 01:04:15,854
Sold it for a bunch of money.

1456
01:04:15,854 --> 01:04:18,750
A month afterwards, I found myself

1457
01:04:18,750 --> 01:04:20,605
in the back of an ambulance on the way

1458
01:04:20,605 --> 01:04:24,728
to a hospital in Hawaii, living in Hawaii at the time,

1459
01:04:24,728 --> 01:04:27,412
with a hundred percent blockage of the widow maker.

1460
01:04:27,412 --> 01:04:28,867
- [Audience Member] Oh no. (audience murmuring)

1461
01:04:28,867 --> 01:04:32,450
- A hundred percent blockage of the widow maker kills 95%

1462
01:04:32,450 --> 01:04:35,490
of the people that that happens to, 95.

1463
01:04:35,490 --> 01:04:37,760
So I'm the lucky 5%.

1464
01:04:37,760 --> 01:04:39,540
I didn't know that was happening.

1465
01:04:39,540 --> 01:04:41,130
They were taking me in for observation,

1466
01:04:41,130 --> 01:04:42,810
that they weren't even gonna turn on the siren.

1467
01:04:42,810 --> 01:04:46,844
When the siren went on, I knew something hit the fan.

1468
01:04:46,844 --> 01:04:49,588
And so, I said at that moment, I said,

1469
01:04:49,588 --> 01:04:51,711
"No, I'm not going.

1470
01:04:51,711 --> 01:04:54,169
I still have stuff to do." (audience chuckling)

1471
01:04:54,169 --> 01:04:55,002
That's what I said.

1472
01:04:55,002 --> 01:04:57,480
If I have to hold my breath for 15 minutes,

1473
01:04:57,480 --> 01:04:58,313
then, I'm gonna do that.

1474
01:04:58,313 --> 01:05:00,660
Whatever it is, they're gonna bring me back.

1475
01:05:00,660 --> 01:05:02,073
I have two stents.

1476
01:05:02,073 --> 01:05:03,869
That's 20 years ago.

1477
01:05:03,869 --> 01:05:07,620
I've had no problem since, so obviously, it all worked.

1478
01:05:07,620 --> 01:05:10,650
Oh gosh, modern science, two stents,

1479
01:05:10,650 --> 01:05:12,931
I left the hospital four days later with two band-aids.

1480
01:05:12,931 --> 01:05:14,040
(audience chuckling)

1481
01:05:14,040 --> 01:05:16,890
Anyway, in the recovery room, I started thinking,

1482
01:05:16,890 --> 01:05:18,633
what did I mean by "stuff?"

1483
01:05:18,633 --> 01:05:20,490
I had already made enough money,

1484
01:05:20,490 --> 01:05:22,168
so my wife didn't need me anymore.

1485
01:05:22,168 --> 01:05:24,843
(audience laughing)

1486
01:05:24,843 --> 01:05:26,865
My kids were already out of college,

1487
01:05:26,865 --> 01:05:28,890
so my kids didn't need me anywhere.

1488
01:05:28,890 --> 01:05:30,660
So I was kind of done with the kind of stuff

1489
01:05:30,660 --> 01:05:33,120
that you normally think that you need to accomplish.

1490
01:05:33,120 --> 01:05:36,180
I should retire and play golf, but that's not me.

1491
01:05:36,180 --> 01:05:38,370
So anyway, I started thinking,

1492
01:05:38,370 --> 01:05:41,859
what is it that's going to upset me

1493
01:05:41,859 --> 01:05:45,240
if I didn't do something about it by the end of my life,

1494
01:05:45,240 --> 01:05:47,280
by the real end of my life?

1495
01:05:47,280 --> 01:05:48,993
And so, I decided to find my missions in life.

1496
01:05:48,993 --> 01:05:51,410
And the first mission came to me

1497
01:05:51,410 --> 01:05:52,680
in the back of the newspaper.

1498
01:05:52,680 --> 01:05:55,650
This is still in the recovery room, it's in Hawaii.

1499
01:05:55,650 --> 01:05:59,004
It's the Hawaii newspaper, the back of the newspaper now,

1500
01:05:59,004 --> 01:06:00,757
by the obituaries, it's like,

1501
01:06:00,757 --> 01:06:02,700
"Oh, by the way, we're going to kill all the coral

1502
01:06:02,700 --> 01:06:05,460
in the world by the end of the century."

1503
01:06:05,460 --> 01:06:07,315
It was like, "What?

1504
01:06:07,315 --> 01:06:08,377
What's causing that?"

1505
01:06:08,377 --> 01:06:09,877
"Ocean acidification."

1506
01:06:09,877 --> 01:06:10,747
"What's causing that?"

1507
01:06:10,747 --> 01:06:11,883
"Carbon dioxide."

1508
01:06:11,883 --> 01:06:13,267
"And what's causing that?"

1509
01:06:13,267 --> 01:06:14,670
"We are."

1510
01:06:14,670 --> 01:06:19,170
So mission number one, end use of carbon-based fuel.

1511
01:06:19,170 --> 01:06:20,700
That's where it started.

1512
01:06:20,700 --> 01:06:23,386
But once I looked into carbon dioxide, I found out,

1513
01:06:23,386 --> 01:06:26,866
oh my gosh, this thing is causing all the climate change.

1514
01:06:26,866 --> 01:06:29,163
It's like, it's a disaster.

1515
01:06:30,090 --> 01:06:32,667
So anyway, that mission,

1516
01:06:32,667 --> 01:06:34,682
end the use of carbon-based fuel,

1517
01:06:34,682 --> 01:06:36,791
can sound like a big mission.

1518
01:06:36,791 --> 01:06:39,960
It can, but it doesn't have to be.

1519
01:06:39,960 --> 01:06:41,283
Just another thing to do.

1520
01:06:42,242 --> 01:06:44,820
I started my first foundation,

1521
01:06:44,820 --> 01:06:47,520
a first non-profit, called it a foundation,

1522
01:06:47,520 --> 01:06:48,930
which is a big mistake,

1523
01:06:48,930 --> 01:06:50,753
'cause everyone thinks you're going to give them money.

1524
01:06:50,753 --> 01:06:51,930
(audience laughing)

1525
01:06:51,930 --> 01:06:53,370
That's not what we do.

1526
01:06:53,370 --> 01:06:54,450
We do stuff.

1527
01:06:54,450 --> 01:06:55,283
Anyway,

1528
01:06:57,030 --> 01:06:58,920
so, and we said about ending the use

1529
01:06:58,920 --> 01:07:00,660
of carbon-based fuel in Hawaii.

1530
01:07:00,660 --> 01:07:04,530
Now, at the time, Hawaii imported $5 billion,

1531
01:07:04,530 --> 01:07:07,425
billion dollars a year of oil

1532
01:07:07,425 --> 01:07:09,771
and a billion dollars of coal.

1533
01:07:09,771 --> 01:07:13,140
Out of the billion dollars, 30% is jet fuel,

1534
01:07:13,140 --> 01:07:15,240
30% is ground transportation,

1535
01:07:15,240 --> 01:07:20,160
40%, or $2 billion, is making electricity.

1536
01:07:20,160 --> 01:07:21,877
So $2 billion, plus a billion,

1537
01:07:21,877 --> 01:07:25,080
$3 billion of fossil fuel was being used

1538
01:07:25,080 --> 01:07:28,239
to make electricity every single year.

1539
01:07:28,239 --> 01:07:30,810
That's a million and a half people, plus tourists,

1540
01:07:30,810 --> 01:07:32,280
plus military.

1541
01:07:32,280 --> 01:07:34,230
So that's a lot of money.

1542
01:07:34,230 --> 01:07:39,056
And that is kind of reproduced on some similar scale

1543
01:07:39,056 --> 01:07:41,350
on every other island in the world.

1544
01:07:41,350 --> 01:07:42,812
In fact, it's worse.

1545
01:07:42,812 --> 01:07:45,803
We import oil, 'cause we have refineries,

1546
01:07:45,803 --> 01:07:49,320
and most islands import diesel, which is already refined,

1547
01:07:49,320 --> 01:07:51,513
which means it's more expensive.

1548
01:07:51,513 --> 01:07:56,513
So anyway, electricity generation was the biggest use

1549
01:07:57,053 --> 01:07:58,350
of fossil fuel sights.

1550
01:07:58,350 --> 01:08:00,030
I said, "We're gonna go after that.

1551
01:08:00,030 --> 01:08:02,154
If we do that one, then, people will see,

1552
01:08:02,154 --> 01:08:05,328
'Hey, we can take on the biggest problem, solve that,

1553
01:08:05,328 --> 01:08:08,370
and then, they can go on and solve the smaller problems

1554
01:08:08,370 --> 01:08:09,870
to give them the confidence.'"

1555
01:08:10,844 --> 01:08:12,360
We tried everything.

1556
01:08:12,360 --> 01:08:15,183
I mean, I knew nothing about this when we started.

1557
01:08:16,922 --> 01:08:19,598
We tried talking to the governor.

1558
01:08:19,598 --> 01:08:21,900
She said, "You know nothing about this."

1559
01:08:21,900 --> 01:08:24,153
We had a female,

1560
01:08:24,153 --> 01:08:27,000
not saying that that had anything to do with it.

1561
01:08:27,000 --> 01:08:28,207
I'm just saying that she said,

1562
01:08:28,207 --> 01:08:29,580
"You don't know what you're talking about."

1563
01:08:29,580 --> 01:08:30,413
And I didn't.

1564
01:08:30,413 --> 01:08:33,450
I said, "I will, I'll learn."

1565
01:08:33,450 --> 01:08:36,203
And same thing with the politicians.

1566
01:08:36,203 --> 01:08:38,310
And then, the electric company wouldn't give us

1567
01:08:38,310 --> 01:08:40,023
the time of day, utility.

1568
01:08:41,576 --> 01:08:42,900
And we tried everything.

1569
01:08:42,900 --> 01:08:45,570
We ended up deciding that we're gonna get the people

1570
01:08:45,570 --> 01:08:46,403
on our side.

1571
01:08:46,403 --> 01:08:48,090
And so, how do you get to the people?

1572
01:08:48,090 --> 01:08:51,063
How did people in this country stop smoking?

1573
01:08:51,063 --> 01:08:53,853
It wasn't because there's a label on the package

1574
01:08:53,853 --> 01:08:55,669
that says that this stuff is gonna kill you.

1575
01:08:55,669 --> 01:08:56,880
(audience chuckling)

1576
01:08:56,880 --> 01:08:58,931
It was because our children asked us to stop.

1577
01:08:58,931 --> 01:09:01,230
There was a campaign to get our children

1578
01:09:01,230 --> 01:09:03,390
to ask their parents to stop smoking.

1579
01:09:03,390 --> 01:09:05,160
So I said, "Okay, the children is the way to go."

1580
01:09:05,160 --> 01:09:07,140
So we had children go door to door

1581
01:09:07,140 --> 01:09:09,369
and exchange 300,000 light bulbs,

1582
01:09:09,369 --> 01:09:12,360
and thereby, having a conversation between a child

1583
01:09:12,360 --> 01:09:15,720
and adult about efficiency, about saving money.

1584
01:09:15,720 --> 01:09:19,824
Because an an LED light bulb uses one 10th the electricity

1585
01:09:19,824 --> 01:09:21,510
of an incandescent light bulb.

1586
01:09:21,510 --> 01:09:22,677
So we traded them,

1587
01:09:22,677 --> 01:09:25,080
and we made bouquets of incandescent light bulb

1588
01:09:25,080 --> 01:09:26,399
and gave them to the politicians.

1589
01:09:26,399 --> 01:09:29,219
(audience chuckling) You know, things like that.

1590
01:09:29,219 --> 01:09:32,866
Eventually, we prevailed, and we passed a law in Hawaii.

1591
01:09:32,866 --> 01:09:35,016
We're the first state in the country

1592
01:09:35,016 --> 01:09:37,200
to have a law that says Hawaii has

1593
01:09:37,200 --> 01:09:39,172
to go a hundred percent renewable.

1594
01:09:39,172 --> 01:09:43,076
We wanted 2040, they were gonna give us 2050.

1595
01:09:43,076 --> 01:09:46,177
We negotiated, and we got 2045.

1596
01:09:46,177 --> 01:09:48,960
That's where the 2045 date came from.

1597
01:09:48,960 --> 01:09:51,204
And my son pointed out to me, you know,

1598
01:09:51,204 --> 01:09:53,370
that happens to be the hundredth anniversary

1599
01:09:53,370 --> 01:09:54,900
of the United Nations.

1600
01:09:54,900 --> 01:09:58,430
So that kind of, yeah, somehow that number came to me.

1601
01:09:58,430 --> 01:10:01,113
It came to us, and it means something.

1602
01:10:03,690 --> 01:10:07,075
So we followed it up by changing the business model

1603
01:10:07,075 --> 01:10:10,260
of the utility, so that they make more money

1604
01:10:10,260 --> 01:10:11,610
by switching to renewables.

1605
01:10:11,610 --> 01:10:13,289
Now, they paid, at that time,

1606
01:10:13,289 --> 01:10:18,289
or still do, maybe more, 25 cents per kilowatt hour for oil,

1607
01:10:18,591 --> 01:10:20,894
25 cents per kilowatt hour.

1608
01:10:20,894 --> 01:10:24,718
When they put out RFPs, requests for proposal

1609
01:10:24,718 --> 01:10:28,170
for renewable energy, wind and solar came in

1610
01:10:28,170 --> 01:10:29,646
at about 8 cents per kilowatt hour.

1611
01:10:29,646 --> 01:10:31,050
- [Audience Member] Oh!

1612
01:10:31,050 --> 01:10:33,702
- And if you add batteries, which you need,

1613
01:10:33,702 --> 01:10:36,487
because wind and solar are intermittent,

1614
01:10:36,487 --> 01:10:38,820
it's up to 12 cents per kilowatt hour.

1615
01:10:38,820 --> 01:10:42,286
That's still less than half of the cost of oil.

1616
01:10:42,286 --> 01:10:44,697
So their old business model was two

1617
01:10:44,697 --> 01:10:47,430
and a half sales, 10% on the price of oil,

1618
01:10:47,430 --> 01:10:50,400
what a dumb ass business model.

1619
01:10:50,400 --> 01:10:52,800
Ten percent on the price of oil means they're happy

1620
01:10:52,800 --> 01:10:54,300
when the price of oil goes up.

1621
01:10:55,858 --> 01:10:57,420
That's two and a half cents.

1622
01:10:57,420 --> 01:11:00,155
We said, "Okay, you make 3 cents on the renewables."

1623
01:11:00,155 --> 01:11:01,695
Guess who's our best friend now?

1624
01:11:01,695 --> 01:11:03,160
(audience chuckling)

1625
01:11:03,160 --> 01:11:06,550
So Hawaii today, that was 10 years ago.

1626
01:11:06,550 --> 01:11:10,179
Today, Hawaii's at 38% renewable energy.

1627
01:11:10,179 --> 01:11:12,933
Some parts are faster, some parts are slower.

1628
01:11:13,879 --> 01:11:16,194
And it's not all easy.

1629
01:11:16,194 --> 01:11:20,220
You know, we're having problems with the government trying

1630
01:11:20,220 --> 01:11:22,050
to bring in LMG at this point,

1631
01:11:22,050 --> 01:11:26,940
which is a, it's not a bridge, it's a pier, in my opinion.

1632
01:11:26,940 --> 01:11:29,907
So anyway, Hawaii's on track.

1633
01:11:29,907 --> 01:11:33,699
Two years later, California copied us.

1634
01:11:33,699 --> 01:11:37,036
They credit us for having come up with the legislation,

1635
01:11:37,036 --> 01:11:39,150
and they passed the same legislation,

1636
01:11:39,150 --> 01:11:41,819
a hundred percent renewable by 2045.

1637
01:11:41,819 --> 01:11:46,572
Followed by Illinois, New York, Washington, Oregon.

1638
01:11:46,572 --> 01:11:50,610
Fifteen other states now have the same legislation.

1639
01:11:50,610 --> 01:11:53,820
So over 50% of the people in this country live

1640
01:11:53,820 --> 01:11:55,614
in a state that is gonna go

1641
01:11:55,614 --> 01:11:57,240
a hundred percent renewable energy

1642
01:11:57,240 --> 01:11:58,073
by 2040,

1643
01:11:59,763 --> 01:12:01,170
2045, 2050.

1644
01:12:01,170 --> 01:12:03,693
It's a little bit different state by state.

1645
01:12:05,592 --> 01:12:07,585
It's working!

1646
01:12:07,585 --> 01:12:08,430
It's working.

1647
01:12:08,430 --> 01:12:09,750
We're halfway there.

1648
01:12:09,750 --> 01:12:13,222
Now, we, of course, focused on the easy half,

1649
01:12:13,222 --> 01:12:15,550
but I mean, that's still, you know,

1650
01:12:15,550 --> 01:12:17,849
let's focus on the easy half.

1651
01:12:17,849 --> 01:12:19,260
Here's the problem.

1652
01:12:19,260 --> 01:12:21,805
The problem is that the United States is only 4.3%

1653
01:12:21,805 --> 01:12:24,115
of the people in the world.

1654
01:12:24,115 --> 01:12:26,883
And so, even if we're a hundred percent renewable energy,

1655
01:12:26,883 --> 01:12:31,883
that still leaves 96% of the rest of the world to go.

1656
01:12:32,628 --> 01:12:35,390
So we decided, well, I decided that we're gonna have

1657
01:12:35,390 --> 01:12:38,348
to do the same thing we did in the US, outside the US.

1658
01:12:38,348 --> 01:12:42,030
So we started the Blue Planet Alliance.

1659
01:12:42,030 --> 01:12:42,870
I moved to New York,

1660
01:12:42,870 --> 01:12:45,420
so I could be close to the United Nations.

1661
01:12:45,420 --> 01:12:48,219
And we started doing the same thing we did

1662
01:12:48,219 --> 01:12:50,520
in the US, outside.

1663
01:12:50,520 --> 01:12:51,660
So how do we do that?

1664
01:12:51,660 --> 01:12:54,360
We bring islands, first of all, islands,

1665
01:12:54,360 --> 01:12:55,957
because they're the ones who suffer the most

1666
01:12:55,957 --> 01:12:57,296
from climate change

1667
01:12:57,296 --> 01:13:00,441
and they pay the highest for electricity.

1668
01:13:00,441 --> 01:13:03,246
So we bring islands, we bring them to Hawaii,

1669
01:13:03,246 --> 01:13:04,650
show them what we did.

1670
01:13:04,650 --> 01:13:07,230
Now what we bring them to Hawaii, it's four people,

1671
01:13:07,230 --> 01:13:09,900
it's someone from the government, someone from the utility,

1672
01:13:09,900 --> 01:13:11,700
someone from the regulatory agency,

1673
01:13:11,700 --> 01:13:13,250
and someone from the community,

1674
01:13:14,160 --> 01:13:16,471
like, an environmentalist.

1675
01:13:16,471 --> 01:13:18,330
No women, no pay.

1676
01:13:18,330 --> 01:13:20,834
Meaning, if there's no woman in the mix,

1677
01:13:20,834 --> 01:13:22,620
we don't pay for their airfare.

1678
01:13:22,620 --> 01:13:24,113
They have to pay their own way.

1679
01:13:24,113 --> 01:13:27,675
Our board is always going to be a majority of women.

1680
01:13:27,675 --> 01:13:30,413
- [Audience Member] Ah. (Henk chuckling)

1681
01:13:30,413 --> 01:13:31,750
- Men got us into this,

1682
01:13:31,750 --> 01:13:33,540
and I have absolutely no confidence

1683
01:13:33,540 --> 01:13:34,551
that men can get us out of this.

1684
01:13:34,551 --> 01:13:39,210
(audience laughing) (audience applauding)

1685
01:13:39,210 --> 01:13:41,786
So yes, we have women on our board,

1686
01:13:41,786 --> 01:13:45,360
and some of them are very vocal and very strong.

1687
01:13:45,360 --> 01:13:48,757
So one woman, no women, no pay.

1688
01:13:48,757 --> 01:13:49,933
(audience chuckling)

1689
01:13:49,933 --> 01:13:53,493
We have so far brought 35 islands,

1690
01:13:54,330 --> 01:13:57,462
Caribbean, sorry, Pacific, Caribbean,

1691
01:13:57,462 --> 01:14:01,470
Indian Ocean Islands, to Hawaii, and two countries.

1692
01:14:01,470 --> 01:14:04,589
And coming up, we do this every six months,

1693
01:14:04,589 --> 01:14:09,390
eight to 10 countries, island countries.

1694
01:14:09,390 --> 01:14:11,460
And we keep them for a week,

1695
01:14:11,460 --> 01:14:13,830
two days where they get to meet everybody

1696
01:14:13,830 --> 01:14:15,366
that was involved in the transition,

1697
01:14:15,366 --> 01:14:18,673
the naysayers, the before and after people.

1698
01:14:18,673 --> 01:14:21,420
Then, in the middle of the week on Wednesday,

1699
01:14:21,420 --> 01:14:24,783
we take them around the island, show them what we've done,

1700
01:14:26,490 --> 01:14:29,078
and I mean, all the renewable island,

1701
01:14:29,078 --> 01:14:31,065
renewable energy projects.

1702
01:14:31,065 --> 01:14:32,970
And then, we spend a couple

1703
01:14:32,970 --> 01:14:34,770
of days teaching them how to work together.

1704
01:14:34,770 --> 01:14:39,704
Then, on Friday, each team gets to present

1705
01:14:39,704 --> 01:14:43,213
what's the solution for their island, you know,

1706
01:14:43,213 --> 01:14:45,030
based on listening to us

1707
01:14:45,030 --> 01:14:46,920
and what they know about their island.

1708
01:14:46,920 --> 01:14:48,270
And then, we send them back.

1709
01:14:48,270 --> 01:14:50,979
And then, we have a team of experts,

1710
01:14:50,979 --> 01:14:52,689
if you wanna call 'em that,

1711
01:14:52,689 --> 01:14:55,971
that have been doing all the presentations.

1712
01:14:55,971 --> 01:14:58,731
They basically are there to support them

1713
01:14:58,731 --> 01:15:00,600
after they go back.

1714
01:15:00,600 --> 01:15:01,893
So they have questions,

1715
01:15:05,275 --> 01:15:07,618
whatever, they run into roadblocks.

1716
01:15:07,618 --> 01:15:09,360
We're having a roadblock in Bermuda right now.

1717
01:15:09,360 --> 01:15:12,630
I was on a Zoom just before this.

1718
01:15:12,630 --> 01:15:15,825
And they have the LNG problem.

1719
01:15:15,825 --> 01:15:18,480
Somebody wants to bring in LNG.

1720
01:15:18,480 --> 01:15:23,480
Okay, so that's the foundation, that's the alliance.

1721
01:15:23,760 --> 01:15:26,873
Oh, by the way, I'm a computer game designer.

1722
01:15:26,873 --> 01:15:28,644
Did I tell you that already? (audience chuckling)

1723
01:15:28,644 --> 01:15:30,060
Yeah, I did.

1724
01:15:30,060 --> 01:15:32,520
So whenever I go to a conference, and we're in a room,

1725
01:15:32,520 --> 01:15:35,160
and we're trying to figure out how to fix climate change,

1726
01:15:35,160 --> 01:15:37,984
somebody comes up with a bright idea, "Let's gamify this."

1727
01:15:37,984 --> 01:15:39,504
(audience laughing)

1728
01:15:39,504 --> 01:15:41,257
And then, Edward looks at me and said,

1729
01:15:41,257 --> 01:15:42,200
"Okay, when are you gonna get to work?"

1730
01:15:42,200 --> 01:15:43,190
- [Audience Member] Yeah.

1731
01:15:43,190 --> 01:15:44,590
- It's like, "Wait, wait, wait.

1732
01:15:44,590 --> 01:15:47,190
I'm not in the game business anymore."

1733
01:15:47,190 --> 01:15:49,829
It's hard enough to get somebody to play a game.

1734
01:15:49,829 --> 01:15:52,350
And if you try to get somebody to play a game

1735
01:15:52,350 --> 01:15:53,460
that has some kind of lesson

1736
01:15:53,460 --> 01:15:55,050
that they're supposed to like, learn,

1737
01:15:55,050 --> 01:15:58,760
and then, apply in the real world, that's like impossible.

1738
01:15:58,760 --> 01:16:00,750
There's too many games out there.

1739
01:16:00,750 --> 01:16:04,404
I remember there was an ad,

1740
01:16:04,404 --> 01:16:05,833
an ad for a game.

1741
01:16:05,833 --> 01:16:07,980
It's like, you play this game,

1742
01:16:07,980 --> 01:16:09,778
and you don't have to learn anything.

1743
01:16:09,778 --> 01:16:11,252
(audience chuckling)

1744
01:16:11,252 --> 01:16:12,900
You don't have to learn anything.

1745
01:16:12,900 --> 01:16:14,190
Like, okay, all right.

1746
01:16:14,190 --> 01:16:16,076
So that's a hard thing, but anyway,

1747
01:16:16,076 --> 01:16:17,490
finally, I gave in.

1748
01:16:17,490 --> 01:16:19,740
I said, "Okay, I'm gonna think about this."

1749
01:16:19,740 --> 01:16:22,979
And so, I'm making,

1750
01:16:22,979 --> 01:16:25,599
pretty soon, it's going into testing.

1751
01:16:25,599 --> 01:16:30,165
I made a role playing game for action.

1752
01:16:30,165 --> 01:16:33,136
You could say climate action, but it's any kind of action.

1753
01:16:33,136 --> 01:16:35,520
Call it the Action App.

1754
01:16:35,520 --> 01:16:37,856
And what the Action App does,

1755
01:16:37,856 --> 01:16:39,660
it answers the question, you know?

1756
01:16:39,660 --> 01:16:41,970
Like, I give a talk like this, and at the end of the talk,

1757
01:16:41,970 --> 01:16:44,550
some people come up to me and say, "So what can I do?"

1758
01:16:44,550 --> 01:16:46,770
And I say, "I don't know what you can do.

1759
01:16:46,770 --> 01:16:50,676
You have to figure out what you can do, and do that."

1760
01:16:50,676 --> 01:16:52,170
You know?

1761
01:16:52,170 --> 01:16:54,134
Everybody's different.

1762
01:16:54,134 --> 01:16:56,940
Figure out what you don't like about the world

1763
01:16:56,940 --> 01:16:58,291
and go and fix that.

1764
01:16:58,291 --> 01:17:00,540
I mean, you don't have to fix what I want you to fix,

1765
01:17:00,540 --> 01:17:02,670
fix what you want you to fix.

1766
01:17:02,670 --> 01:17:03,540
And then, I realized

1767
01:17:03,540 --> 01:17:05,153
that I really haven't helped them at all.

1768
01:17:05,153 --> 01:17:08,103
They're just as puzzled about what they should do next

1769
01:17:08,103 --> 01:17:11,319
as they were before they asked me the question.

1770
01:17:11,319 --> 01:17:14,671
So the Action App is a list of things to do.

1771
01:17:14,671 --> 01:17:16,950
And in the beginning, they're tiny things.

1772
01:17:16,950 --> 01:17:18,666
They're pick up a piece of rubbish,

1773
01:17:18,666 --> 01:17:19,650
put it in the rubbish cans,

1774
01:17:19,650 --> 01:17:21,450
switch the lights, turn off the light switch

1775
01:17:21,450 --> 01:17:23,150
in a room where there's no people.

1776
01:17:25,140 --> 01:17:25,987
And you do a bunch of those small things,

1777
01:17:25,987 --> 01:17:27,690
and then, you go up a level.

1778
01:17:27,690 --> 01:17:29,220
That's role playing. (audience chuckling)

1779
01:17:29,220 --> 01:17:30,582
Now, you're level two.

1780
01:17:30,582 --> 01:17:34,050
And level two means you get to do bigger things.

1781
01:17:34,050 --> 01:17:37,152
In Dungeon and Dragons, you get to fight bigger monsters.

1782
01:17:37,152 --> 01:17:38,970
But these are bigger things to do.

1783
01:17:38,970 --> 01:17:40,160
And you know, maybe the things

1784
01:17:40,160 --> 01:17:42,447
in the beginning take a minute, then, they take 10 minutes.

1785
01:17:42,447 --> 01:17:46,009
Level three maybe takes an hour, and then, so on.

1786
01:17:46,009 --> 01:17:47,960
By the time you're up to level five or six,

1787
01:17:47,960 --> 01:17:50,325
you're doing things that take a week.

1788
01:17:50,325 --> 01:17:52,275
And what do you get for it?

1789
01:17:52,275 --> 01:17:54,066
You get status.

1790
01:17:54,066 --> 01:17:56,550
Status, I mean, when you think about it,

1791
01:17:56,550 --> 01:17:58,977
everything we do, the money,

1792
01:17:58,977 --> 01:18:01,259
and so on, it's all about status.

1793
01:18:01,259 --> 01:18:05,040
Bigger car, better television, farther vacation,

1794
01:18:05,040 --> 01:18:07,800
bigger lawn, bigger house, bigger this,

1795
01:18:07,800 --> 01:18:10,476
bigger that, more expensive, this or that.

1796
01:18:10,476 --> 01:18:12,570
And these are all status symbols.

1797
01:18:12,570 --> 01:18:14,670
So let's go straight to the status.

1798
01:18:14,670 --> 01:18:17,340
And the way I want the status to work is

1799
01:18:17,340 --> 01:18:21,491
when Taylor Swift goes to have a concert somewhere,

1800
01:18:21,491 --> 01:18:24,150
on Monday, the level sixes get to buy tickets,

1801
01:18:24,150 --> 01:18:27,630
on Tuesday, level five, and then, Wednesday level four,

1802
01:18:27,630 --> 01:18:28,860
and so on.

1803
01:18:28,860 --> 01:18:32,040
The same price, but the people who have done the most

1804
01:18:32,040 --> 01:18:33,390
for the environment

1805
01:18:33,390 --> 01:18:37,020
or have taken the most action will be sitting in the front.

1806
01:18:37,020 --> 01:18:38,155
So you're...

1807
01:18:38,155 --> 01:18:42,990
And then, basically, the artist, or the sports team,

1808
01:18:42,990 --> 01:18:47,096
or the stadium can take credit for having caused

1809
01:18:47,096 --> 01:18:51,045
all those people to take all that action.

1810
01:18:51,045 --> 01:18:53,310
So there's two ways that you can make points.

1811
01:18:53,310 --> 01:18:54,660
One is you can do the actions,

1812
01:18:54,660 --> 01:18:56,996
but two is you can create an action.

1813
01:18:56,996 --> 01:18:58,440
When you create an action

